  "note": "The functions kept in this game's derived client library. Its server library is the starting point, because most engine functions exist on both VMs under the same name — but most of it is NOT client-side, and shipping the whole thing offers completions for functions the client VM does not have.",
  "unverified": "NONE OF THIS IS DOCUMENTATION-VERIFIED, and it cannot be: this game ships no documentation for its client VM, which is the reason the library is derived at all. Every name here is kept on circumstantial evidence, and a name being absent is not proof the client VM lacks it. Black Ops 3 is the only game whose client library is hand-documented — t7_api_csc.json is a real source and is never generated or pruned by this tool.",
  "rule": "Two independent kinds of evidence, either sufficient. called: this game's own shipped .csc scripts call the name, which is direct proof the client VM has it. t7: Black Ops 3's hand-documented client library lists it, which is a later engine in the same Treyarch line saying the function is client-side. Neither subsumes the other — see the counts below — so the union is used. Measured on BO3, where the true answer is known, keeping only corpus-called names would have discarded 28.6% of functions that are certainly client-side, which is what the second criterion is there to recover.",
